In Java, Enums (short for Enumerations) are a special data type that allows you to define a collection of constants.
An enum type is a special class that represents a group of constants (unchangeable variables, like final variables).
Enums are used to represent a fixed set of related constants, making the code more readable and type-safe.

Key Features of Enums:
Type Safety: Enums provide type safety by restricting the values to a predefined set.
Singleton Nature: Each value of an enum is a singleton, meaning there is only one instance of each constant.
Built-in Methods: Java enums have built-in methods like values(), valueOf(), and others that can be used to work with enum constants.
Can Have Fields, Methods, and Constructors: Enums can have fields, methods, and constructors, making them more
powerful than just a collection of constants

public enum Day {
    MONDAY, TUESDAY, WEDNESDAY, THURSDAY, FRIDAY, SATURDAY, SUNDAY;
}
public class TestEnum {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Day today = Day.MONDAY;

        switch (today) {
            case MONDAY:
                System.out.println("Start of the workweek!");
                break;
            case FRIDAY:
                System.out.println("End of the workweek!");
                break;
            default:
                System.out.println("Midweek day.");
        }
    }
}

public enum Day {
    MONDAY("Start of workweek"),
    TUESDAY("Second day of the week"),
    WEDNESDAY("Midweek"),
    THURSDAY("Almost Friday"),
    FRIDAY("End of workweek"),
    SATURDAY("Weekend"),
    SUNDAY("Weekend, relaxing day");

    private String description;

    // Constructor to initialize the field
    Day(String description) {
        this.description = description;
    }

    // Method to retrieve the description
    public String getDescription() {
        return this.description;
    }
}

public class TestEnum {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Day today = Day.MONDAY;
        System.out.println("Today is " + today + ": " + today.getDescription());
    }
}

1. values(): Returns an array of all enum constants.
Day[] days = Day.values();
for (Day day : days) {
    System.out.println(day);
}
2. valueOf(String name): Returns the enum constant with the specified name (case-sensitive).
Day day = Day.valueOf("MONDAY"); // Returns MONDAY
3. ordinal(): Returns the position of the enum constant in its declaration (0-based)
System.out.println(Day.MONDAY.ordinal()); // Prints 0


Enums can also have methods to perform logic related to the constants.
public enum Day {
    MONDAY, TUESDAY, WEDNESDAY, THURSDAY, FRIDAY, SATURDAY, SUNDAY;

    public boolean isWeekend() {
        return this == SATURDAY || this == SUNDAY;
    }
}

public class TestEnum {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Day today = Day.SATURDAY;
        if (today.isWeekend()) {
            System.out.println("It's the weekend!");
        } else {
            System.out.println("It's a weekday.");
        }
    }
}

